# Adding Event Listeners (`addEventListener`) 👂

The modern standard way to attach an event handler to a DOM node:

```javascript_not
const btn = document.querySelector('#submit-btn');

btn.addEventListener('click', function(event) {
  console.log("Button clicked!");
});
```

## Why `addEventListener` is Superior to `onclick`:
1. Allows attaching **multiple independent listeners** to the exact same event.
2. Supports fine-grained controls (options like `{ once: true }` and capturing).
3. Works cleanly with `removeEventListener` for memory management.

# Common Event Types 📋

| Category | Event Name | When it Triggers |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Mouse** | `click`, `dblclick`, `mouseenter`, `mouseleave` | User clicks or moves mouse |
| **Keyboard** | `keydown`, `keyup` | User presses or releases a key |
| **Form** | `submit`, `input`, `change`, `focus`, `blur` | Form actions, text changes, focus |
| **Window** | `load`, `DOMContentLoaded`, `resize`, `scroll` | Page ready, viewport resize, scrolling |

```javascript_not
// Triggered on every keystroke in a search input
searchInput.addEventListener('input', (e) => {
  console.log("User typed:", e.target.value);
});
```

# The Event Object (`e`) 🎯

When an event fires, the browser automatically passes an **Event Object** as the first argument to your handler callback:

```javascript_not
btn.addEventListener('click', (event) => {
  console.log("Event type:", event.type);          // "click"
  console.log("Target element:", event.target);     // The clicked button
  console.log("Mouse X coordinate:", event.clientX);
  console.log("Mouse Y coordinate:", event.clientY);
});
```

For keyboard events:
```javascript_not
window.addEventListener('keydown', (e) => {
  console.log("Key pressed:", e.key); // e.g. "Enter", "Escape"
});
```

# Preventing Default Actions (`preventDefault`) 🛑

Many HTML elements have built-in default browser behaviors:
- Submitting a form reloads the page.
- Clicking an `<a>` link navigates to a new URL.
- Right-clicking opens the browser context menu.

Call `event.preventDefault()` to stop the browser's default behavior:

```javascript_not
const form = document.querySelector('#login-form');

form.addEventListener('submit', (e) => {
  e.preventDefault(); // Prevents page reload!

  // Now submit form data asynchronously via fetch API!
  console.log("Form validated and ready for async AJAX submit!");
});
```

# Stopping Propagation (`stopPropagation`) ✋

Sometimes you want to handle an event on a child element without allowing it to bubble up to parent containers:

```javascript_not
childBtn.addEventListener('click', (e) => {
  e.stopPropagation(); // Stops the event from bubbling up to parent cards!
  console.log("Child button handled.");
});

parentCard.addEventListener('click', () => {
  console.log("Parent card clicked!"); // Will NOT fire when childBtn is clicked!
});
```

# Event Delegation: High-Performance Architecture 🚀

Imagine you have a list of 1,000 items. Attaching 1,000 separate event listeners wastes huge amounts of device memory!

**Event Delegation** attaches a **single listener** to the parent container and leverages event bubbling:

```html_not
<ul id="todo-list">
  <li>Buy Groceries <button class="delete">✕</button></li>
  <li>Walk Dog <button class="delete">✕</button></li>
</ul>
```

```javascript_not
const todoList = document.getElementById('todo-list');

todoList.addEventListener('click', (e) => {
  // Check if the clicked target was a delete button
  if (e.target.classList.contains('delete')) {
    const item = e.target.closest('li');
    item.remove();
  }
});
```

# Dynamic Elements with Event Delegation 🪄

If you add new `<li>` items to the list later via JavaScript, event delegation **automatically works on the new items** without needing to attach new listeners!

```javascript_not
// Add a new item dynamically
const newItem = document.createElement('li');
newItem.innerHTML = 'Write Code <button class="delete">✕</button>';
todoList.append(newItem);

// The delete button on newItem works IMMEDIATELY because the parent listener catches its bubble!
```

# Removing Event Listeners (`removeEventListener`) 🧹

To prevent memory leaks when components unmount, remove event listeners:

```javascript_not
function handleWindowResize() {
  console.log("Resized to:", window.innerWidth);
}

// 1. Add listener
window.addEventListener('resize', handleWindowResize);

// 2. Remove listener (must reference the exact same named function!)
window.removeEventListener('resize', handleWindowResize);
```

> ⚠️ **Anonymous Function Warning:** You **cannot** remove an anonymous arrow function (`() => {}`) because its reference is lost! Always use a named function if you intend to remove it later.

# Advanced Listener Options ⚙️

`addEventListener` accepts a third options object parameter:

```javascript_not
btn.addEventListener('click', handleClick, {
  once: true,    // Listener fires exactly ONCE and automatically unbinds itself!
  passive: true, // Guarantees handler will never call preventDefault() (Boosts scroll performance!)
  capture: true  // Fires during the Capturing phase instead of Bubbling phase
});
```

`passive: true` is crucial for `touchstart` and `scroll` events on mobile to eliminate touch lag!

# Keyboard Shortcuts & Keyboard Navigation ⌨️

Create keyboard shortcuts with `keydown`:

```javascript_not
window.addEventListener('keydown', (e) => {
  // Check for Escape key to close modals
  if (e.key === "Escape") {
    closeModal();
  }

  // Check for Cmd + K or Ctrl + K (Search shortcut)
  if ((e.metaKey || e.ctrlKey) && e.key.toLowerCase() === "k") {
    e.preventDefault(); // Stop default browser search bar
    openQuickSearch();
  }
});
```

# DOMContentLoaded vs Window Load ⏱️

- **`DOMContentLoaded`**: Fires when HTML markup is completely parsed into the DOM tree. External images, stylesheets, and subframes may still be downloading. **(Best time to run UI scripts!)**
- **`window.load`**: Fires only after the HTML, all external images, fonts, and stylesheets are **100% downloaded**.

```javascript_not
document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => {
  console.log("DOM is fully parsed and ready for manipulation!");
});
```

# Performance: Debouncing Input Events ⏱️

Typing fast in a search input can fire 20 events per second. **Debouncing** delays execution until the user stops typing for a set duration:

```javascript_not
function debounce(func, delay = 300) {
  let timer;
  return (...args) => {
    clearTimeout(timer);
    timer = setTimeout(() => func(...args), delay);
  };
}

const handleSearch = (e) => {
  console.log("Searching API for:", e.target.value);
};

searchInput.addEventListener('input', debounce(handleSearch, 300));
```

# Custom Events (`CustomEvent`) 📢

Components can emit custom application events:

```javascript_not
const userCart = document.querySelector('#cart');

// 1. Create a custom event with custom payload data
const cartUpdateEvent = new CustomEvent('cartUpdated', {
  detail: { itemCount: 3, total: 89.99 }
});

// 2. Dispatch the event
userCart.dispatchEvent(cartUpdateEvent);

// 3. Listen for the custom event elsewhere
document.addEventListener('cartUpdated', (e) => {
  console.log(`Cart now has ${e.detail.itemCount} items!`);
});
```
